SUMMARY:Fat Studies & Health at Every Size (Online)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T173000
DTSTAMP:20251002T154627Z
UID:20260425T160000Z-371530@www.lclark.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T090000
ATTACH:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/80/height/80/crop/1/
 src_region/435,0,2566,2129/96367_pexels-bich-tran-916023.rev.1666829452.jp
 g
DESCRIPTION:This course explores the interdisciplinary field of scholarshi
 p in Fat Studies that aims to debunk weight-centric misconceptions and cou
 ntering mainstream narratives.  Participants will examine fat bias\, fat s
 hame and weight-based oppression as a social justice issue that intersects
  with other systems of oppression.  This course includes an introduction t
 o the paradigm of Health At Every Size (HAES)\, a weight-neutral approach 
 to health promotion that emphasizes size diversity\, intuitive eating\, an
 d joyful movement. HAES is also an important paradigm in working with peop
 le who experience disordered eating.  This course is part of our Eating Di
 sorders Certificate Program (https://graduate.lclark.edu/programs/continui
 ng_education/certificates/eating_disorders/).&#160\;Course Details &amp\; 
 RegistrationDates &amp\; Times: Saturday-Sunday\, April 25-26\, 2026\, 9 a
 .m.-5:30 p.m.\, PT\, OnlineInstructor: Krystal Marcinkiewicz\, MA\, LPC\, 
 LMHCCurrent Students and Certificate Candidates: CECP 835-02\, 1 semester 
 hour of Graduate Continuing Education Credit\, $375. Contact ccecerts@lcla
 rk.edu (mailto:ccecerts@lclark.edu) with questions.&#160\;Individual Class
 : We welcome students seeking to take individual courses within the certif
 icate program. Admission to the program is required. Click here for more i
 nformation (https://www.lclark.edu/programs/continuing_education/certifica
 tes/eating_disorders/#Admission)We are committed to making our events acce
 ssible to all needs and abilities. If you have a disability that may impac
 t your academic performance\, you may request accommodations by meeting wi
 th the Office of Student Accessibility staff and submitting documentation 
 on the Office of Student Accessibility website (/offices/student-accessibi
 lity/). Please be aware that the process for evaluation and implementation
  of accommodations could take 2-3 weeks\, so please be in touch with the O
 SA as soon as possible. Email access@lclark.edu (mailto:access@lclark.edu)
  with any additional questions or concerns.About the InstructorKrystal Mar
 cinkiewicz\, MA\, LPC\, LMHC is a Licensed Professional Counselor and Clin
 ical Supervisor\, having earned her Masters Degree in Professional Mental 
 Health Counseling from Lewis &amp\; Clark College in 2018. Drawing from an
  integrative feminist approach\, her work is grounded in person-centered v
 alues\, emphasizing authentic connection\, and collaborative engagement. H
 er primary clinical focus is supporting clients in navigating identity dev
 elopment\, body politics\, neurodivergence\, emotional codependency\, high
 ly sensitive personalities and other complex emotional experiences.  In ad
 dition to clinical work\, Krystal is also passionate in training and mento
 ring emerging counselors. She is an adjunct professor\, teaching a wide va
 riety of graduate counseling courses with an underlying emphasis on counse
 lor identity development\, reflective practice\, and cultivating professio
 nal resilience.  In both the classroom\, and her clinical work\, Krystal i
 s committed to creating affirming spaces that honor intersectional identit
 ies and lived experience.New workshops and trainings are added to our cale
 ndar regularly. For the latest on professional development related to your
  specific interests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfafor
 ms.net/4735441)
LAST-MODIFIED:20260423T184022Z
LOCATION:Online
URL:https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/371530-fat-studies-healt
 h-at-every-size-online
