BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lewis Clark": "https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/390030-leadership-societies-exclusive-cle-professor-yin-on-p"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lewis_clark
X-SOURCE:Lewis Clark
SUMMARY:Leadership Societies Exclusive CLE: Professor Yin on "Protecting J
 udicial Independence From Populist Threats"
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260505T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260505T130000
DTSTAMP:20260410T114628Z
UID:20260505T190000Z-390030@www.lclark.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260505T120000
ATTACH:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/32/width/80/height/80/crop/1/
 src_region/0,0,3200,3200/112752_Tung_Yin_square.rev.1775846783.jpg
DESCRIPTION:"Protecting Judicial Independence From Populist Threats"  An i
 ndependent judiciary is a critical part of a free society. Yet\, populist 
 leaders such as Trump\, Orban\, and others have sought to or have actually
  weakened their judiciaries. It is one thing to declare the principle that
  judges should be independent of other branches of government. It is anoth
 er to take steps to protect the independence of the judiciary from populis
 t threats.  This presentation provides an overview of such threats to judi
 cial independence\, along with ways in which a liberal democracy might str
 ucture its government to provide maximum protection to its judges – and 
 the ways in which those protections might nevertheless fail. Specifically\
 , the presentation covers (1) different threats that judges might face\, f
 rom retaliation in the form of loss of job\, to personal harm\, to dilutio
 n of judicial votes\, (2) ways in which the U.S. Constitution and federal 
 statutes respond to these potential threats\, and tactics that populist mo
 vements might nevertheless use to overcome those structural safeguards\, a
 nd (3) the possibility that transnational courts\, such as the European Co
 urt of Human Rights\, might play a key role in backstopping a nation's own
  laws\, though such transnational courts also face challenges in fulfillin
 g that role.  Professor Yin will speak for approximately 40 minutes\, foll
 owed by a q/a period. 1.0 General CLE (pending approval). Event RSVP by in
 vitation only.Professor Tung Yin: (https://www.lclark.edu/live/profiles/27
 1-tung-yin-)  Tung Yin joined the Lewis &amp\; Clark Law School faculty in
  2009. Before that\, he taught for seven years at The University of Iowa C
 ollege of Law\, where he was most recently professor and Claire Ferguson C
 arlson Faculty Fellow\; and practiced law from 1998-2002 with Munger Tolle
 s &amp\; Olson LLP in Los Angeles\, where he specialized in white-collar c
 orporate criminal defense and employment law. He clerked for the late Hon.
  Edward Rafeedie\, U.S. District Court for the Central District of Califor
 nia\, the late Hon. William J. Holloway\, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Te
 nth Circuit\, and the Hon. J. Clifford Wallace\, U.S. Court of Appeals for
  the Ninth Circuit. While in law school at the University of California\, 
 Berkeley\, he was a Notes and Comments Editor of the California Law Review
  and a member of the Moot Court Board.  Yin's academic research focuses pr
 imarily on national security and terrorism law and has ranged from legal i
 ssues arising out of indefinite military detention of suspected terrorists
  at Guantanamo Bay to race and religion and the perception of terrorism\, 
 to drone terrorism\, and more. His scholarship has been cited in judicial 
 opinions from the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth and Ninth Circuits\,
  the Florida and Georgia Supreme Courts\, and other lower state and federa
 l trial courts. In 2025\, he published National Security Lies\, a monograp
 h examining presidential lies about national security matters.  He frequen
 tly provides commentary for local and national media on high-profile crimi
 nal matters\, including news outlets such as The Wall Street Journal\, The
  New York Times\, The Los Angeles Times\, Associated Press\, The New York 
 Daily News\, Bloomberg News\, The National Law Journal\, The Oregonian\, B
 loomberg Radio\, Oregon Public Broadcasting\, KEX News\, KXL News\, KPAM N
 ews\, "The Lars Larson Show\," and the local news affiliates for ABC\, CBS
 \, NBC\, and Fox.  His foreign languages include Mandarin (limited working
  proficiency) and Japanese (elementary proficiency).
LAST-MODIFIED:20260410T215236Z
LOCATION:Zoom
URL:https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/390030-leadership-societ
 ies-exclusive-cle-professor-yin-on-p
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="font-weight: 400\;">  "Protecting J
 udicial Independence From Populist Threats"</p><p style="font-weight: 400\
 ;">  An independent judiciary is a critical part of a free society. Yet\, 
 populist leaders such as Trump\, Orban\, and others have sought to or have
  actually weakened their judiciaries. It is one thing to declare the princ
 iple that judges should be independent of other branches of government. It
  is another to take steps to protect the independence of the judiciary fro
 m populist threats.</p><p>  This presentation provides an overview of such
  threats to judicial independence\, along with ways in which a liberal dem
 ocracy might structure its government to provide maximum protection to its
  judges – and the ways in which those protections might nevertheless fai
 l. Specifically\, the presentation covers (1) different threats that judge
 s might face\, from retaliation in the form of loss of job\, to personal h
 arm\, to dilution of judicial votes\, (2) ways in which the U.S. Constitut
 ion and federal statutes respond to these potential threats\, and tactics 
 that populist movements might nevertheless use to overcome those structura
 l safeguards\, and (3) the possibility that transnational courts\, such as
  the European Court of Human Rights\, might play a key role in backstoppin
 g a nation's own laws\, though such transnational courts also face challen
 ges in fulfilling that role.</p><p>  Professor Yin will speak for approxim
 ately 40 minutes\, followed by a q/a period. 1.0 General CLE (pending appr
 oval). Event RSVP by invitation only.</p><p><a href="https://www.lclark.ed
 u/live/profiles/271-tung-yin-" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Professor Tu
 ng Yin:</a></p><p>  Tung Yin joined the Lewis &amp\; Clark Law School facu
 lty in 2009. Before that\, he taught for seven years at The University of 
 Iowa College of Law\, where he was most recently professor and Claire Ferg
 uson Carlson Faculty Fellow\; and practiced law from 1998-2002 with Munger
  Tolles &amp\; Olson LLP in Los Angeles\, where he specialized in white-co
 llar corporate criminal defense and employment law. He clerked for the lat
 e Hon. Edward Rafeedie\, U.S. District Court for the Central District of C
 alifornia\, the late Hon. William J. Holloway\, U.S. Court of Appeals for 
 the Tenth Circuit\, and the Hon. J. Clifford Wallace\, U.S. Court of Appea
 ls for the Ninth Circuit. While in law school at the University of Califor
 nia\, Berkeley\, he was a Notes and Comments Editor of the <em>California 
 Law Review</em> and a member of the Moot Court Board.</p><p>  Yin's academ
 ic research focuses primarily on national security and terrorism law and h
 as ranged from legal issues arising out of indefinite military detention o
 f suspected terrorists at Guantanamo Bay to race and religion and the perc
 eption of terrorism\, to drone terrorism\, and more. His scholarship has b
 een cited in judicial opinions from the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fift
 h and Ninth Circuits\, the Florida and Georgia Supreme Courts\, and other 
 lower state and federal trial courts. In 2025\, he published <em>National 
 Security Lies</em>\, a monograph examining presidential lies about nationa
 l security matters.</p><p>  He frequently provides commentary for local an
 d national media on high-profile criminal matters\, including news outlets
  such as <em>The Wall Street Journal\, The New York Times\, The Los Angele
 s Times\, Associated Press\, The New York Daily News\, Bloomberg News\, Th
 e National Law Journal</em>\, <em>The Oregonian</em>\, Bloomberg Radio\, O
 regon Public Broadcasting\, KEX News\, KXL News\, KPAM News\, "The Lars La
 rson Show\," and the local news affiliates for ABC\, CBS\, NBC\, and Fox.<
 /p><p>  His foreign languages include Mandarin (limited working proficienc
 y) and Japanese (elementary proficiency).</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:390030
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/32/width/80/height
 /80/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,3200\,3200/112752_Tung_Yin_square.rev.17758467
 83.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Tung Yin
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<div class="gmail_default">\n  Join us for an hour wit
 h Professor Tung Yin\, who is giving a 1-CLE webinar on "Protecting Judici
 al Independence From Populist Threats". The webinar is open to all members
  of Lewis &amp\; Clark Law School's <a href="https://www.lclark.edu/giving
 /leadership-annual-giving-societies/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">leade
 rship giving societies</a>\, Bacon Associates\, Advocates and Gantenbein S
 ociety.\n</div>
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/Los_Angeles
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
END:VEVENT
