BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lewis Clark": "https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/381635-4-at-5-lecture-series-featuring-pamplin-professor-eme"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lewis_clark
X-SOURCE:Lewis Clark
SUMMARY:4 at 5 Lecture Series Featuring Pamplin Professor Emeritus Stephen
  Dow Beckham – The Pig War and Series Closing Reception
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260513T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260513T190000
DTSTAMP:20260127T170855Z
UID:20260514T000000Z-381635@www.lclark.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260513T170000
ATTACH:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/45/width/80/height/80/crop/1/
 src_region/0,0,854,854/111391_Beckham-Stephen-20251007-TRPL-Board-Headshot
 -WEB.jpg_1.jpg
CATEGORIES:Open to the Public
DESCRIPTION:The Pig War: The San Juan Islands in ConflictFollowed by Lectu
 re Series Closing Reception  In-person at L&amp\;CLindner Auditorium\, Mil
 ler Center for Humanities (Miller 105)Livestream hosted by the L&amp\;C Wa
 shington Alumni Chapter  Livestream link shared upon registration  Wednesd
 ay\, May 13\, 2026  5:00 - 7:00 P.M. (in-person)  5:00 - 6:00 P.M. (online
 )Click to Register (https://engage.lclark.edu/register/?id=c195d64c-93d8-4
 b90-b942-106ba20ba031)Times for those joining remotely from other time zon
 es:8 - 9 P.M. Eastern ● 7 - 8 P.M Central ● 6 - 7 P.M. Mountain  4 - 5
  P.M. Alaska ● 2 - 3 P.M. Hawaii  This lecture series takes place the se
 cond Wednesday of every month\, February-May.About the Lecture  Professor 
 Beckham presents "The Pig War: San Juan Islands in Conflict\, 1859–1872\
 ," a lecture detailing the international controversy in Puget Sound that n
 early brought the United States and Great Britain to war. This historical 
 event resonates with modern tensions regarding the 49th parallel boundary\
 , tariffs\, and sovereign rights.  Prof. Beckham examines how the killing 
 of a pig on San Juan Island precipitated an outrageous explosion of confli
 ct over the exact location of the boundary between Canada and the United S
 tates. The "Pig War" led to a significant build-up of army and navy forces
  and the construction of fortifications\, with tensions only ultimately re
 solved through international arbitration by Kaiser Wilhelm I of Germany.  
 Using historical photographs\, watercolors\, and maps\, Prof. Beckham plac
 es the San Juan Islands conflict within the broader context of the America
 n Civil War\, boundary disputes in the Stikine River watershed\, fisheries
  off the coast of Nova Scotia\, and American claims against England regard
 ing the sinking of Union vessels by the British-built\, Confederate-operat
 ed CSS Alabama.About the PresenterStephen Dow Beckham is Pamplin Professor
  Emeritus of History at Lewis &amp\; Clark College\, where he served for f
 orty-two years. A preeminent authority on the American West and the Lewis 
 &amp\; Clark Expedition\, Professor Beckham dedicated the majority of his 
 career to teaching undergraduate students in the College of Arts and Scien
 ces\, while also contributing his expertise to the Law School's Indian Law
  Program.  Renowned for his immersive teaching\, he famously led field-bas
 ed courses retracing the Lewis &amp\; Clark trail on horseback and sailing
  the Northwest Coast. Beyond the classroom\, he has served as an expert wi
 tness in over forty tribal rights cases and currently chairs the Oregon Co
 mmittee on Historic Preservation. A former Oregon Professor of the Year an
 d recipient of the American Historical Association's Asher Distinguished T
 eaching Award\, Professor Beckham also serves on the board of the Theodore
  Roosevelt Presidential Library\, continuing his lifelong commitment to hi
 storical scholarship and conservation.  &#160\;
GEO:45.450858;-122.668265
LAST-MODIFIED:20260129T184901Z
LOCATION:Miller Hall\, Room 105 (Lindner Auditorium)
URL:https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/381635-4-at-5-lecture-se
 ries-featuring-pamplin-professor-eme
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>The P
 ig War: The San Juan Islands in Conflict</strong></p><p style="text-align:
  center\;"><strong>Followed by Lecture Series Closing Reception<br /><br /
 >  In-person at L&amp\;C<br /></strong>Lindner Auditorium\, Miller Center 
 for Humanities (Miller 105)</p><p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Liv
 estream hosted by the L&amp\;C Washington Alumni Chapter</strong><br />  L
 ivestream link shared upon registration<strong><br /></strong></p><p style
 ="text-align: center\;">  Wednesday\, May 13\, 2026<br />  5:00 - 7:00 P.M
 . (in-person)<br />  5:00 - 6:00 P.M. (online)</p><p style="text-align: ce
 nter\;"><strong><a class="btn-small-style-1 btn-small-style-1" href="https
 ://engage.lclark.edu/register/?id=c195d64c-93d8-4b90-b942-106ba20ba031" ta
 rget="_blank" rel="noopener">Click to Register</a></strong></p><p style="t
 ext-align: center\;"><strong>Times for those joining remotely from other t
 ime zones:<br /></strong>8 - 9 P.M. Eastern ● 7 - 8 P.M Central ● 6 - 
 7 P.M. Mountain<br />  4 - 5 P.M. Alaska ● 2 - 3 P.M. Hawaii</p><p style
 ="text-align: left\;">  This lecture series takes place the second Wednesd
 ay of every month\, February-May.</p><p><strong>About the Lecture</strong>
 </p><p data-path-to-node="3">  Professor Beckham presents <strong data-pat
 h-to-node="3" data-index-in-node="35">"The Pig War: San Juan Islands in Co
 nflict\, 1859–1872\,"</strong> a lecture detailing the international con
 troversy in Puget Sound that nearly brought the United States and Great Br
 itain to war. This historical event resonates with modern tensions regardi
 ng the 49th parallel boundary\, tariffs\, and sovereign rights.</p><p data
 -path-to-node="4">  Prof. Beckham examines how the killing of a pig on San
  Juan Island precipitated an outrageous explosion of conflict over the exa
 ct location of the boundary between Canada and the United States. The "Pig
  War" led to a significant build-up of army and navy forces and the constr
 uction of fortifications\, with tensions only ultimately resolved through 
 international arbitration by Kaiser Wilhelm I of Germany.</p><p data-path-
 to-node="5">  Using historical photographs\, watercolors\, and maps\, Prof
 . Beckham places the San Juan Islands conflict within the broader context 
 of the American Civil War\, boundary disputes in the <strong data-path-to-
 node="5" data-index-in-node="183">Stikine River</strong> watershed\, fishe
 ries off the coast of Nova Scotia\, and American claims against England re
 garding the sinking of Union vessels by the British-built\, Confederate-op
 erated <strong data-path-to-node="5" data-index-in-node="366">CSS Alabama<
 /strong>.</p><p><strong>About the Presenter</strong></p><p data-path-to-no
 de="1"><strong data-path-to-node="1" data-index-in-node="0">Stephen Dow Be
 ckham</strong> is Pamplin Professor Emeritus of History at Lewis &amp\; Cl
 ark College\, where he served for forty-two years. A preeminent authority 
 on the American West and the Lewis &amp\; Clark Expedition\, Professor Bec
 kham dedicated the majority of his career to teaching undergraduate studen
 ts in the College of Arts and Sciences\, while also contributing his exper
 tise to the Law School's Indian Law Program.</p><p data-path-to-node="2"> 
  Renowned for his immersive teaching\, he famously led field-based courses
  retracing the Lewis &amp\; Clark trail on horseback and sailing the North
 west Coast. Beyond the classroom\, he has served as an expert witness in o
 ver forty tribal rights cases and currently chairs the Oregon Committee on
  Historic Preservation. A former Oregon Professor of the Year and recipien
 t of the American Historical Association's Asher Distinguished Teaching Aw
 ard\, Professor Beckham also serves on the board of the Theodore Roosevelt
  Presidential Library\, continuing his lifelong commitment to historical s
 cholarship and conservation.</p><p style="text-align: center\;">  &#160\;<
 /p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  alumni@lclark.edu\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:381635
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/45/width/80/height
 /80/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,854\,854/111391_Beckham-Stephen-20251007-TRPL-
 Board-Headshot-WEB.jpg_1.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Join us for the <em><strong>4 at 5 Lecture Seri
 es Featuring Pamplin Professor Emeritus Stephen Dow Beckham</strong></em>\
 , an exciting collection of four history lectures presented at 5:00 P.M. t
 he second Wednesday of each month. This series offers an historical deep d
 ive into people and events associated with a variety of geographical regio
 ns with the aim of bringing our community together from across the nation 
 to embark on a journey of discovery\, bridging the past and present throug
 h the expert guidance of one of our institution's most cherished academic 
 luminaries.\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:alumni weekend|alumni|history|political science|washingto
 n chapter
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/Los_Angeles
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
END:VEVENT
