BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lewis Clark": "https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/367385-beatrice-m-cannady-graduation-celebration"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lewis_clark
X-SOURCE:Lewis Clark
SUMMARY:Beatrice M. Cannady Graduation Celebration
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T110000
DTSTAMP:20250312T111648Z
UID:20260515T170000Z-367385@www.lclark.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T100000
ATTACH:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/32/width/80/height/80/crop/1/
 src_region/0,0,308,388/107704_Beatrice_M._Cannady.rev.1741803379.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Beatrice M. Cannady was the first African-American woman to gr
 aduate from Lewis &amp\; Clark Law School\, and the first Black woman law 
 graduate in the entire state of Oregon. This event is dedicated to her to 
 honor her remarkable contributions to the legal community and community wr
 it large. Cannady was part of the campaign to repeal Oregon&#8217\;s laws 
 denying people of color the right to vote and outlawing Black people from 
 living in the state\, and she helped write Oregon's first civil rights leg
 islation. She was the city's unofficial ambassador between the races\, est
 ablishing the NAACP's Portland branch. This graduation celebration is to i
 nspire our graduates to follow in this trailblazer's footsteps in creating
  positive change and advocating for justice. Cannady's legacy reminds us t
 o envision a more just future in the face of adversity and dedicate oursel
 ves to bringing it to fruition.  If you are a student graduating in 2025\,
  please RSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/14ysC5vVxeufJCMQD4d8t4y
 4V3kroHtySSSTRs-fPVN0/edit) by Friday\, April 11\, 2025\, if you would lik
 e to be honored at this event.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260414T194040Z
LOCATION:Amphitheater
URL:https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/367385-beatrice-m-cannad
 y-graduation-celebration
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div><i>Beatrice M. Cannady was the first Afr
 ican-American woman to graduate from Lewis &amp\; Clark Law School\, and t
 he first Black woman law graduate in the entire state of Oregon. This even
 t is dedicated to her to honor her remarkable contributions to the legal c
 ommunity and community writ large. Cannady was part of the campaign to rep
 eal Oregon&#8217\;s laws denying people of color the right to vote and out
 lawing Black people from living in the state\, and she helped write Oregon
 's first civil rights legislation. She was the city's unofficial ambassado
 r between the races\, establishing the NAACP's Portland branch. This gradu
 ation celebration is to inspire our graduates to follow in this trailblaze
 r's footsteps in creating positive change and advocating for justice. Cann
 ady's legacy reminds us to envision a more just future in the face of adve
 rsity and dedicate ourselves to bringing it to fruition.</i></div><div></d
 iv><div></div><div>  If you are a student graduating in 2025\, please <a h
