X-SOURCE-ID:ananda_portland
X-SOURCE:Ananda Portland
UID:5004090@anandaportland.org
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260626T063000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260626T080000
DTSTAMP:20251217T194207Z
URL:https://anandaportland.org/events/morning-group-meditation-2025-12-17-
 2025-12-18-2025-12-19-2025-12-22-2025-12-23-2025-12-24-2025-12-26-2025-12-
 29-2025-12-30-2025-12-31-2026-01-01-2026-01-02-2026-01-05-2026-01-06-2026-
 01-25-2026-06-26/
SUMMARY:Morning Group Meditation
DESCRIPTION:Weekday mornings we offer a group meditation in the beautiful s
 anctuary of our temple.  Each morning is led by a different member of our
  ministry team with the following flow:\n\n6:15 - doors are open\n\n6:30 -
  opening prayer\, chanting\, reading from Whispers from Eternity by Paramh
 ansa Yogananda\n\n6:40 approx - silent meditation begins\n\n6:45 - front d
 oor is locked\n\n7:55 - 8: 00  -  meditation ends with healing prayers\n
 \n&nbsp\;\n\nYou are able to leave at anytime if you cannot stay for the e
 ntire meditation.  We have chairs and a few blankets and cushions availab
 le but you are encouraged to bring your own meditation gear. \n\n&nbsp\;\
 n\n\n\n&nbsp\;\n\n"Group meditation is like a castle which protects spirit
 ual novices as well as veteran meditators against the besieging armies of 
 delusion. Meditating together increases the degree of Self-realization of 
 each member of the group\, by the law of invisible vibratory exchange of s
 piritual magnetism."   Paramhansa Yogananda
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://anandaportland.org/wp-content/uploads/20
 22/02/Group-Meditation-4.jpg
CATEGORIES:Meditation
LOCATION:Ananda Temple in Beaverton\, 4855 SW Watson Avenue\, Beaverton\, O
 regon\, 97005\, United States
X-APPLE-STRUCTURED-LOCATION;VALUE=URI;X-ADDRESS=4855 SW Watson Avenue\, Bea
 verton\, Oregon\, 97005\, United States;X-APPLE-RADIUS=100;X-TITLE=Ananda 
 Temple in Beaverton:geo:0,0
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lewis Clark": "https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/387513-applied-suicide-intervention-skills-training-asist-in"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lewis_clark
X-SOURCE:Lewis Clark
SUMMARY:Applied Suicide Intervention Skills Training (ASIST) [In-Person]
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260626T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260626T163000
DTSTAMP:20260319T094028Z
UID:20260626T153000Z-387513@www.lclark.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260626T083000
ATTACH:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/80/height/80/crop/1/
 src_region/241,0,1524,1282/112486_huyngan-sunset-9949027_1920.rev.17739388
 53.jpg
DESCRIPTION:The Applied Suicide Intervention Skills Training (ASIST) is an
  intensive\, interactive and practice-dominated course for those who want 
 to feel more comfortable\, confident and competent in providing an interve
 ntion with someone who is having thoughts of suicide.&#160\;This workshop 
 will provide extensive opportunities for supported skills practice and sha
 red participant experiences that enrich learning.After this workshop\, par
 ticipants will have an increased ability to:      Recognize when someone m
 ay be thinking about suicide          Provide a skilled intervention\, inc
 luding connect the person to further support          Guide the developmen
 t of a safety plan through a collaborative\, helping relationship         
  Consider how personal and community attitudes about suicide affect someon
 e's openness to seek help and the caregiver's ability to provide it       
    Reduce suicide stigma and build confidence and capacity to provide suic
 ide first aidWho should attend? The training is for anyone in a position o
 f trust\, including mental health and addictions professionals\, school ps
 ychologists\, school counselors\, educators\, healthcare workers\, peer su
 pport and recovery specialists\, housing specialists\, law enforcement\, f
 aith leaders\, and community volunteers.  "Prior to participating in ASIST
 \, I was reluctant to get involved in conversations involving suicide beca
 use I felt uneasy about how to engage effectively. I walked away from this
  training with clear and applicable tools to enter a conversation with con
 fidence and fearlessness\, equipped to support and converse openly about s
 uicide. It was absolutely one of the most valuable trainings I have experi
 enced in my career."  - Counselor\, Roosevelt High SchoolTraining credits 
 are NASP and NASW-approved for Continuing Professional Development (CPD).D
 etails &amp\; RegistrationDates and Time: Friday &amp\; Saturday\, June 26
 -27\, 2026\, 8:30 a.m.-4:30 p.m.&#160\;Location: Lewis &amp\; Clark Gradua
 te School\, South Chapel and York 107Instructors: Leslie Rodgers\, and Kat
 hy Wilson-FeyCost and Credits: $350\, includes 15 NASP-Approved CEUs or PD
 Us (12.75 NASW Credits). Alumni\, Adjuncts\, and GSEC Clinical Mental Heal
 th Supervisors save 20%.&#160\;Register Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.ne
 t/wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002Unrl)Accessibility: We are committed to making o
 ur events accessible to all needs and abilities. When registering\, please
  let us know your access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.e
 du (mailto:cce@lclark.edu)&#160\;with questions.Please note: Discounted &#
 x2018\;Student Rate&#x2019\; registrations are for current students only a
 nd do not include continuing education credit (CEU/PDUs)About the Instruct
 orsLeslie Rodgers is a licensed clinical social worker and registered ASIS
 T (Applied Suicide Intervention Skills) trainer with over 20 years experie
 nce working with children and adults in various therapeutic settings\, inc
 luding group homes\, psychiatric hospitals\, and schools. Her current work
  has involved implementing best practice procedures and providing staff de
 velopment in student threat assessment\, suicide prevention\, and crisis r
 esponse for school districts. She holds a BA from the University of Texas 
 and a Masters in Social Work from Portland State University.Kathy Wilson-F
 ey is a registered ASIST Trainer and has clinical therapy and teaching exp
 erience in a variety of settings\, including community college\, community
  mental health\, and an international university. She has 15 years of expe
 rience as a mental health specialist in public schools\, where she worked 
 with students and families\, trained school staff on suicide intervention\
 , and coordinated school support during crises. Kathy is fluent in Spanish
  and is committed to equity work and cultural competence. She holds a BA f
 rom Whitman College and a Masters in Counseling Psychology from the Univer
 sity of Oregon.New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regul
 arly. For the latest on professional development related to your specific 
 interests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/473
 5441)
LAST-MODIFIED:20260330T213737Z
