ATTACH:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/80/height/80/crop/1/
 src_region/0,0,3200,2400/107142_pexels-taufiq-m-1266928452-30522934.rev.17
 38798635.jpg
DESCRIPTION:As clinicians\, we need to be prepared to help clients deconst
 ruct how their bodies have been storied.  This includes raising awareness 
 of\, and challenging\, the impact of societal and relational power dynamic
 s. These dynamics impact not only our inner dialog\, but the everyday real
 ities of our lives.  This course explores how we evaluate our bodies throu
 gh the lens of "unidentified others\," as well as the lived experience and
  consequences of looks\, privilege and discrimination.  Participants will 
 consider liberation-based counseling practices to counter the objectificat
 ion\, commodification\, power and gendering of bodies in late capitalist s
 ocieties.  The focus of this course is to raise awareness of body politics
  in the clinical practice and creating opportunities for change through in
 dividual and relational wellbeing.  This course is part of our Eating Diso
 rders Certificate Program (https://graduate.lclark.edu/programs/continuing
 _education/certificates/eating_disorders/).Course Details &amp\; Registrat
 ionDates and Times: Saturday-Sunday\, June 27-28\, 2026\, 9 a.m.-3:50 p.m.
 \, PT\, OnlineInstructor:&#160\;Krystal Marcinkiewicz\, MA\, LPC\, LMHCCur
 rent Students and Certificate Candidates: CECP 837-1\, 1 semester hour of 
 Graduate Continuing Education credit\, $385.&#160\;Individual Class: We we
 lcome students seeking to take individual courses within the certificate p
 rogram. Admission to the program is required. Click here for more informat
 ion (https://www.lclark.edu/programs/continuing_education/certificates/eat
 ing_disorders/#Admission)Register Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735
 425)We are committed to making our events accessible to all needs and abil
 ities. If you have a disability that may impact your academic performance\
 , you may request accommodations by meeting with the Office of Student Acc
 essibility staff and submitting documentation on the Office of Student Acc
 essibility website (/offices/student-accessibility/). Please be aware that
  the process for evaluation and implementation of accommodations could tak
 e 2-3 weeks\, so please be in touch with the OSA as soon as possible. Emai
 l access@lclark.edu (mailto:access@lclark.edu) with any additional questio
 ns or concerns.About the Instructor Krystal Marcinkiewicz\, MA\, LPC\, LMH
 C is a Licensed Professional Counselor and Clinical Supervisor\, having ea
 rned her Masters Degree in Professional Mental Health Counseling from Lewi
 s &amp\; Clark College in 2018. Drawing from an integrative feminist appro
 ach\, her work is grounded in person-centered values\, emphasizing authent
 ic connection\, and collaborative engagement. Her primary clinical focus i
 s supporting clients in navigating identity development\, body politics\, 
 neurodivergence\, emotional codependency\, highly sensitive personalities 
 and other complex emotional experiences.  In addition to clinical work\, K
 rystal is also passionate in training and mentoring emerging counselors. S
 he is an adjunct professor\, teaching a wide variety of graduate counselin
 g courses with an underlying emphasis on counselor identity development\, 
 reflective practice\, and cultivating professional resilience.  In both th
 e classroom\, and her clinical work\, Krystal is committed to creating aff
 irming spaces that honor intersectional identities and lived experience.Ne
 w workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularly. For the lat
 est on professional development related to your specific interests\, sign 
 up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
LAST-MODIFIED:20260323T183859Z
