UID:20260711T160000Z-375748@www.lclark.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T090000
ATTACH:https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/80/height/80/crop/1/
 src_region/0,0,1920,1280/97566_wild-flowers-863437_1920.rev.1675207469.jpg
DESCRIPTION:As clinicians\, we need to be prepared to help clients deconst
 ruct how their bodies have been storied.  This includes raising awareness 
 of\, and challenging\, the impact of societal and relational power dynamic
 s. These dynamics impact not only our inner dialog\, but the everyday real
 ities of our lives.  This course explores how we evaluate our bodies throu
 gh the lens of "unidentified others\," as well as the lived experience and
  consequences of looks\, privilege and discrimination.  Participants will 
 consider liberation-based counseling practices to counter the objectificat
 ion\, commodification\, power and gendering of bodies in late capitalist s
 ocieties.  The focus of this course is to raise awareness of body politics
  in the clinical practice and creating opportunities for change through in
 dividual and relational wellbeing.  This course is part of our Eating Diso
 rders Certificate Program (https://graduate.lclark.edu/programs/continuing
 _education/certificates/eating_disorders/).Course Details &amp\; Registrat
 ionDates and Times: Saturday &amp\; Sunday\, July 11 &amp\; 12\, 2026\, 9 
 a.m.-5:30 p.m.\, PT\, OnlineInstructor: Krystal Marcinkiewicz\, MA\, LPC\,
  LMHCCurrent Students and Certificate Candidates: CECP 837-2\, 1 semester 
 hour of Graduate Continuing Education credit\, $385.&#160\;Individual Clas
 s: We welcome students seeking to take individual courses within the certi
 ficate program. Admission to the program is required. Click here for more 
 information (https://www.lclark.edu/programs/continuing_education/certific
 ates/eating_disorders/#Admission)Register Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.
 net/4735425)We are committed to making our events accessible to all needs 
 and abilities. If you have a disability that may impact your academic perf
 ormance\, you may request accommodations by meeting with the Office of Stu
 dent Accessibility staff and submitting documentation on the Office of Stu
 dent Accessibility website (/offices/student-accessibility/). Please be aw
 are that the process for evaluation and implementation of accommodations c
 ould take 2-3 weeks\, so please be in touch with the OSA as soon as possib
 le. Email access@lclark.edu (mailto:access@lclark.edu) with any additional
  questions or concerns.About the Instructor Krystal Marcinkiewicz\, MA\, L
 PC\, LMHC is a Licensed Professional Counselor and Clinical Supervisor\, h
 aving earned her Masters Degree in Professional Mental Health Counseling f
 rom Lewis &amp\; Clark College in 2018. Drawing from an integrative femini
 st approach\, her work is grounded in person-centered values\, emphasizing
  authentic connection\, and collaborative engagement. Her primary clinical
  focus is supporting clients in navigating identity development\, body pol
 itics\, neurodivergence\, emotional codependency\, highly sensitive person
 alities and other complex emotional experiences.  In addition to clinical 
 work\, Krystal is also passionate in training and mentoring emerging couns
 elors. She is an adjunct professor\, teaching a wide variety of graduate c
 ounseling courses with an underlying emphasis on counselor identity develo
 pment\, reflective practice\, and cultivating professional resilience.  In
  both the classroom\, and her clinical work\, Krystal is committed to crea
 ting affirming spaces that honor intersectional identities and lived exper
 ience.New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularly. For
  the latest on professional development related to your specific interests
 \, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
LAST-MODIFIED:20260323T183929Z
LOCATION:Online
URL:https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/375748-body-politics-onl
 ine
